1 ON

   Says what a command procedure does when one of its commands fails,
   or when Ctrl/Y is pressed while it runs.

   Format

     ON condition THEN [$] command

2 Parameters

 condition

   One of:

     WARNING        A warning, an error, or a fatal error.
     ERROR          An error or a fatal error.
     SEVERE_ERROR   A fatal error.
     CONTROL_Y      Ctrl/Y pressed while the procedure runs.

 command

   The command to run in place of the procedure's usual action, such as
   EXIT, CONTINUE, GOTO, or STOP.

2 Description

   Without an ON, a procedure ends when a command fails with an error
   or a fatal error, and goes on after a warning.

   The command runs once: after it, the usual action is back until the
   next ON.  ON CONTROL_Y is the exception: its command runs for each
   Ctrl/Y until another ON CONTROL_Y, SET NOCONTROL=Y, or the end of the
   procedure.  If an image is running when Ctrl/Y is pressed, it is
   stopped first; a command of DCL's own completes first, and the
   procedure goes on after it unless the command (GOTO, EXIT, STOP)
   goes elsewhere.

   ON applies only to the command level it is in, so a procedure the
   procedure runs has its own.  SET NOON turns the checking off, and SET
   ON (or an ON) back on.  At the terminal, ON is accepted and does
   nothing.

2 Examples

   1.$ ON WARNING THEN EXIT

     Ends the procedure at any failure, a warning included.

   2.$ ON ERROR THEN CONTINUE

     Goes on after the next error.

   3.$ ON CONTROL_Y THEN GOTO CLEAN_UP

     Goes to CLEAN_UP whenever Ctrl/Y is pressed.
